  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry and mild weather through midweek, next system to arrive\non Thursday. \n\nQuiet weather will continue through the overnight, with some\nhigh clouds moving through the area. These clouds have slightly\ndecreased outgoing radiation, keeping temperatures a few degrees\nwarmer than previously forecast. Southeastern West Virginia\ncontinues to hang on to low clouds that are slowly dissipating\nand should be gone by sunrise. After a chilly start to the day\nlater this morning, with lows in the 30s, skies will clear and \na a beautiful fall day is in store, with highs in the low to mid\n60s. \n\nHigh pressure will continue to dominate the synoptic weather \npattern for the next several days, keeping dry and mild weather \nin place for the Mid-Atlantic. As the high shifts east into the \nAtlantic for the early part of the week, high temperatures will\nrise to around 10-15 degrees above normal for this time of \nyear, in the 60s to low 70s for Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nThe next chance of precipitation will arrive on Thursday, as a\nlongwave upper-level trough and a surface low pressure system \nwill develop across the Mississippi Valley midweek and track \nnortheast into the Ohio River Valley on Thursday. Models have\nremained fairly consistent with the strength of the trough,\nencompassing most of the Eastern CONUS Friday into next weekend.\nThe system will bring a chance of rainfall to the area, though\nmodels do differ in the amount of moisture that will be\naccompanying the system. \n\nOnce the front moves through, potentially the coldest air of \nthe season so far moves into our area, as northwest flow takes \nover and the upper trough briefly stalls. Should this scenario \noccur, the western mountains could very well see the first \nsnowflakes of the year. However, still being nearly a week out,\ndetails will likely change. A hard freeze will also be possible\nfor a good chunk of the area, though the growing season has \nalready ended.",
